.ct-header
  display: flex
  padding: 0 0.25em;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;

#isonim-debug-controls
  // The caption bar's left region drags; the control row inside it does not.
  //
  // This element is not the toolbar — it is the bar's left THIRD.  `#menu` gives
  // it `flex: 1 1 0` so it takes an equal share of the leftover space with the
  // session tab bar, which is what centres the command prompt between them (see
  // components/menu_bar.styl).  So it is as wide as the tab strip opposite —
  // ~887px on this display — while the buttons it holds come to ~520px.
  //
  // Marking this container `no-drag` therefore froze the whole 887px, including
  // the ~367px of empty bar after the last button.  That is why the gap left of
  // the command prompt would not move the window while the identical gap on its
  // right would: the tab bar opposite has had the other arrangement all along
  // (components/session_tabs.styl — "Empty bar area is draggable; child
  // tabs/buttons override with no-drag").
  //
  // The opt-out is scoped to the direct child rather than to the buttons so the
  // hole is exactly the control row: it then tracks the row's width by itself
  // when buttons are added or removed, and the gaps between buttons stay
  // non-draggable, so missing a button by a pixel does not drag the window.
  -webkit-app-region: drag
  display: flex
  align-items: center
  align-self: center
  flex: 0 0 auto
  order: 2

  > *
    -webkit-app-region: no-drag

  .isonim-debug-controls
    height: 2.1em
    gap: 0.5em
    padding: 0

    .debug-toolbar-mode,
    .recording-head-indicator
      display: inline-flex
      align-items: center
      height: 100%
      padding: 0 0.35em
      font-size: 0.8em
      line-height: 1
      white-space: nowrap
      color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

    .recording-head-indicator
      color: PRIMARY_COLOR

    .jump-to-live-debug
      min-width: 2.8em
      padding: 0 0.35em
      font-size: 0.78em
      line-height: 1

  // The EDIT-mode surface, mounted into this same host whenever the session is
  // not replaying (`currentTopbarSurface` in ui/debug.nim).  A sibling of the
  // block above, not a child of it — the two panels never coexist.
  //
  // `views/isonim_edit_mode_toolbar_view` gives it `.ct-header` and a leading
  // `.separate-bar` on purpose, "so the topbar keeps one look across a mode
  // switch" — but the look it was matching lives in `.isonim-debug-controls`,
  // and this panel does not carry that class.  It therefore inherited half the
  // shared appearance and none of the spacing: `.ct-header` sets no `gap`, so
  // four bordered pills rendered edge to edge with each button's 1px border
  // touching its neighbour's, drawing a 2px seam between them.  A row of buttons
  // welded into a broken segmented control is the shape the report was pointing
  // at.
  //
  // `height` is the caption bar's own control height rather than the debugger
  // strip's 2.1em.  `#menu` (components/menu_bar.styl) is 2.125em/34px with
  // 0.25em of padding, and its comment fixes 1.625rem/26px as the size that
  // leaves "4px above and 4px below, both whole pixels" — which is what the
  // command prompt and the session tabs already use.  Matching it means every
  // control in the bar shares one height and one centre line; 2.1em (33.6px)
  // would not fit the 26px content box at all.
  //
  // In `rem` for the same reason `#menu`'s `gap` is: these buttons inherit the
  // user agent's 13.33px button font-size, not the bar's 16px, so an `em` here
  // would resolve against a different number than the one the bar was measured
  // in and land at 21px.
  .edit-mode-toolbar
    height: 1.625rem
    gap: 0.5em
    padding: 0
    // `.ct-header` centres its children; the buttons are meant to FILL the row
    // so their borders describe the bar's control height rather than a second,
    // smaller one floating inside it.
    align-items: stretch

    .edit-toolbar-button
      height: 100%
      // `button` sets `flex: 0 0 1.75em !important` for the icon buttons that
      // rule was written for.  On a text button that is a 1.75em flex-basis in
      // the MAIN axis — a width — and the only reason the labels are not all
      // clipped to 23px is the automatic minimum size floor that `min-width:
      // auto` puts under a flex item.  A width that depends on a floor rather
      // than on the declared basis is a width nobody can predict from reading
      // the rule, so state the intent: size to the label.
      flex: 0 0 auto !important
